Here is an example of how you can manage sessions using Google OAuth in a React application using classes instead of effects and Material Design:
```
import React, { Component } from 'react';
import { GoogleLogin } from 'react-google-login';
import { Grid, Typography, Button } from '@material-ui/core';

class SessionManager extends Component {
  constructor(props) {
    super(props);
    this.state = {
      userManager: null,
      token: null,
      expiresAt: null,
      user: null,
    };
  }

  handleLogin = (response) => {
    const { token, expires_at } = response.credential;
    this.setState({
      userManager: new google.auth.GoogleAuth({
        client_id: 'YOUR_CLIENT_ID',
        client_secret: 'YOUR_CLIENT_SECRET',
        redirect_uri: 'YOUR_REDIRECT_URI',
      }),
      token,
      expiresAt: expires_at,
    });
  };

  handleLogout = () => {
    this.setState({
      userManager: null,
      token: null,
      expiresAt: null,
      user: null,
    });
  };

  checkLoginStatus = () => {
    if (this.state.userManager) {
      this.state.userManager.authorize(null, (err, access_token) => {
        if (err) {
          console.error(err);
        } else {
          this.setState({
            token: access_token,
          });
        }
      });
    }
  };

  render() {
    const { userManager, token, expiresAt, user } = this.state;

    return (
      <Grid container justify="center">
        {userManager ? (
          <Grid item xs={6}>
            <Typography variant="h5">You are logged in!</Typography>
            <Button onClick={this.handleLogout}>Logout</Button>
          </Grid>
        ) : (
          <Grid item xs={6}>
            <GoogleLogin
              clientId="YOUR_CLIENT_ID"
              buttonText="Login with Google"
              onSuccess={this.handleLogin}
              onFailure={this.handleLogin}
              cookiePolicy="single_host_origin"
            />
          </Grid>
        )}
      </Grid>
    );
  }
}

export default SessionManager;
```
In this example, we create a `SessionManager` class that manages the Google OAuth login and session. We use the `react-google-login` library to handle the login and logout functionality.

When the user clicks the "Login with Google" button, the `handleLogin` method is called with the response from Google OAuth. We use the `google.auth` library to create a new `GoogleAuth` object with the client ID, client secret, and redirect URI. We then set the `userManager` state to this object, as well as the `token` and `expiresAt` states.

When the user clicks the "Logout" button, the `handleLogout` method is called, which sets the `userManager`, `token`, `expiresAt`, and `user` states to null.

In the `render` method, we check the `userManager` state to determine whether the user is logged in or not. If they are logged in, we display a message and a logout button. If they are not logged in, we display the login button.

You will need to replace `YOUR_CLIENT_ID`, `YOUR_CLIENT_SECRET`, and `YOUR_REDIRECT_URI` with your own Google OAuth client ID, client secret, and redirect URI, respectively.

You can use this component in your React app by rendering it in a `div`:
```
<div>
  <SessionManager />
</div>
```
